3.  **Completeness Trap (Combining Constraints)**: After deriving constraints from different cases (e.g., $p \equiv 1 \pmod{43}$ and $p \not\equiv 1 \pmod{43}$), one might fail to combine them correctly. Both constraints must hold, so `n` must be a multiple of their LCM.
